%[text] # Cooling Coffee: Modeling Temperature Change
%[text] In this activity, you will use measured coffee temperature data to model cooling over time and compare how different cup scenarios affect the time it takes coffee to reach a target drinking temperature.
%[text] **Learning goal:** Learn how to model cooling data using MATLAB.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%[text] - Explore the measured data
%[text] - Fit a cooling model
%[text] - Compare cup scenarios
%[text] - Animate the cooling process
%[text] - Summarize the result \
%%
%[text] ## Opening Question
%[text] A cup of coffee starts at 186 deg F in a room at 71 deg F. If 140 deg F is a reasonable target drinking temperature, how long should you wait before drinking it?
%[text] Write a prediction before running the model: I predict the coffee will reach 140 deg F after about  ***minutes because*** .
%%
%[text] ## Measured Coffee Data
%[text] The measurements below were collected from a cooling cup of coffee. The room temperature was 71 deg F.
timeMinutes = [0 1 2 3 8 13 18 23 28 33 38 43 48]';
coffeeTempF = [186 179 175 171 156 144 135 127 120 116 111 107 104]';
roomTempF = 71;
coffeeData = table(timeMinutes,coffeeTempF,VariableNames=["TimeMinutes","CoffeeTempF"]) %[output:36fe6b68]
plot(timeMinutes,coffeeTempF,"o-","LineWidth",1.5,MarkerFaceColor=[0.72 0.26 0.12]) %[output:636c5ef6]
grid on %[output:636c5ef6]
xlabel("Time (minutes)") %[output:636c5ef6]
ylabel("Coffee temperature (deg F)") %[output:636c5ef6]
title("Measured Coffee Temperature") %[output:636c5ef6]
subtitle("The temperature decreases quickly at first, then more slowly") %[output:636c5ef6]
%%
%[text] ## Fit A Cooling Model
%[text] Newton's law of cooling models the temperature as approaching room temperature over time.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ T(t) = T\_{room} + (T\_0 - T\_{room})e^{-kt} $
%[text] The code estimates the cooling rate $ k $ from the measured data and predicts when the coffee reaches the target temperature.
targetTempF = 140;
temperatureGapF = coffeeTempF - roomTempF;
fitCoefficients = polyfit(timeMinutes,log(temperatureGapF),1);
coolingRatePerMin = -fitCoefficients(1);
initialGapEstimateF = exp(fitCoefficients(2));
modelTime = linspace(0,max(timeMinutes),200);
modelTempF = roomTempF + initialGapEstimateF*exp(-coolingRatePerMin*modelTime);
minutesToTarget = -log((targetTempF - roomTempF)/initialGapEstimateF)/coolingRatePerMin;
temperatureAfter30F = roomTempF + initialGapEstimateF*exp(-coolingRatePerMin*30);
plot(timeMinutes,coffeeTempF,"o",modelTime,modelTempF,"-","LineWidth",1.5) %[output:5c5864d0]
grid on %[output:5c5864d0]
xlabel("Time (minutes)") %[output:5c5864d0]
ylabel("Coffee temperature (deg F)") %[output:5c5864d0]
title("Measured Data And Fitted Cooling Model") %[output:5c5864d0]
legend("Measured data","Cooling model",Location="northeast") %[output:5c5864d0]
yline(targetTempF,":","Target drinking temperature") %[output:5c5864d0]
modelSummary = table(coolingRatePerMin,minutesToTarget,temperatureAfter30F,VariableNames=["CoolingRatePerMin","MinutesTo140F","TemperatureAfter30MinF"]) %[output:082bd0eb]
%[text] The fitted model predicts that this coffee reaches 140 deg F after the estimated number of minutes shown in the table.
%%
%[text] ## Compare Cup Scenarios
%[text] The measured data describe one cup of coffee. The scenarios below use that fitted cooling rate as a baseline, then change the rate to explore what might happen with different cup types. These scenario values are illustrative and should not be treated as product specifications.
scenarioSettings = table(["Regular mug";"Insulated mug";"Paper cup"],["Regular";"Insulated";"Paper"],[1.00;0.55;1.30],["Baseline scenario based on the measured data";"Slower cooling because insulation reduces heat transfer";"Faster cooling in this simplified classroom model"],VariableNames=["Scenario","ShortLabel","RateMultiplier","Interpretation"]);
scenarioSettings.CoolingRatePerMin = coolingRatePerMin*scenarioSettings.RateMultiplier;
scenarioTime = (0:0.5:60)';
scenarioTempF = roomTempF + (coffeeTempF(1) - roomTempF).*exp(-scenarioTime*scenarioSettings.CoolingRatePerMin');
scenarioMinutesToTarget = log((coffeeTempF(1) - roomTempF)/(targetTempF - roomTempF))./scenarioSettings.CoolingRatePerMin;
scenarioTempAfter30F = roomTempF + (coffeeTempF(1) - roomTempF).*exp(-30*scenarioSettings.CoolingRatePerMin);
scenarioResults = table(scenarioSettings.Scenario,scenarioSettings.CoolingRatePerMin,scenarioMinutesToTarget,scenarioTempAfter30F,VariableNames=["Scenario","CoolingRatePerMin","MinutesTo140F","TemperatureAfter30MinF"]) %[output:804b752a]
colororder(scenarioPalette(height(scenarioSettings))) %[output:462ada0e]
plot(scenarioTime,scenarioTempF,LineWidth=1.8) %[output:59c10212]
grid on %[output:59c10212]
xlabel("Time (minutes)") %[output:59c10212]
ylabel("Coffee temperature (deg F)") %[output:59c10212]
title("Cooling Scenario Comparison") %[output:59c10212]
subtitle("Different cooling rates lead to different wait times") %[output:59c10212]
legend(scenarioSettings.Scenario,Location="northeast") %[output:59c10212]
yline(targetTempF,":","Target drinking temperature") %[output:59c10212]
%[text] Compare the scenarios. Which one reaches 140 deg F first? Which one stays warmest after 30 minutes?
%%
%[text] ## Animate The Cooling Process
%[text] The animation below shows the same scenario comparison as a changing visual display.
animationTime = 0:1:60;
animationTemps = roomTempF + (coffeeTempF(1) - roomTempF).*exp(-scenarioSettings.CoolingRatePerMin.*animationTime);
lineColors = scenarioPalette(height(scenarioSettings));
tiledlayout(2,1,TileSpacing="compact",Padding="compact")
temperatureAxes = nexttile;
colororder(temperatureAxes,lineColors)
plot(temperatureAxes,animationTime,animationTemps',LineWidth=1.8)
hold(temperatureAxes,"on")
cursorLine = xline(temperatureAxes,0,"-",LineWidth=1.2,Color=[0.15 0.15 0.15]);
yline(temperatureAxes,targetTempF,":","Target drinking temperature")
hold(temperatureAxes,"off")
grid(temperatureAxes,"on")
xlabel(temperatureAxes,"Time (minutes)")
ylabel(temperatureAxes,"Coffee temperature (deg F)")
title(temperatureAxes,"Animated Cooling Comparison")
legend(temperatureAxes,scenarioSettings.ShortLabel,Location="northeast")
cupAxes = nexttile;
hold(cupAxes,"on")
axis(cupAxes,"equal")
axis(cupAxes,"off")
cupCenters = 1.6 + (0:height(scenarioSettings)-1)*2.6;
xlim(cupAxes,[0.3 cupCenters(end)+1.4])
ylim(cupAxes,[0 3.3])
coffeeFill = gobjects(height(scenarioSettings),1);
cupLabels = gobjects(height(scenarioSettings),1);
for scenarioIndex = 1:height(scenarioSettings)
    centerX = cupCenters(scenarioIndex);
    coffeeFill(scenarioIndex) = patch(cupAxes,centerX + [-0.45 0.45 0.35 -0.35],[0.35 0.35 1.75 1.75],coffeeTemperatureColor(animationTemps(scenarioIndex,1),roomTempF,coffeeTempF(1)),EdgeColor="none");
    patch(cupAxes,centerX + [-0.65 0.65 0.50 -0.50],[0.25 0.25 2.10 2.10],"white",FaceColor="none",EdgeColor=[0.25 0.25 0.25],LineWidth=1.4)
    text(cupAxes,centerX,2.45,scenarioSettings.ShortLabel(scenarioIndex),HorizontalAlignment="center",FontWeight="bold")
    cupLabels(scenarioIndex) = text(cupAxes,centerX,0.03,"",HorizontalAlignment="center");
end
timeLabel = text(cupAxes,mean(cupCenters),3.05,"Elapsed time: 0 min",HorizontalAlignment="center",FontWeight="bold");
hold(cupAxes,"off")
for frameIndex = 1:numel(animationTime)
    currentTime = animationTime(frameIndex);
    currentTemps = animationTemps(:,frameIndex);
    cursorLine.Value = currentTime;
    timeLabel.String = sprintf("Elapsed time: %.0f min",currentTime);
    for scenarioIndex = 1:height(scenarioSettings)
        coffeeFill(scenarioIndex).FaceColor = coffeeTemperatureColor(currentTemps(scenarioIndex),roomTempF,coffeeTempF(1));
        cupLabels(scenarioIndex).String = sprintf("%.0f F",currentTemps(scenarioIndex));
    end
    drawnow
    pause(0.02)
end
scenarioResults(:,["Scenario","MinutesTo140F","TemperatureAfter30MinF"])
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] The measured coffee data follow a cooling pattern where the temperature approaches room temperature. In the scenario comparison, a larger cooling rate reaches the target temperature sooner, while a smaller cooling rate keeps the coffee hotter for longer.
%[text] A limitation is that the cup scenarios are simplified. To make a stronger claim about a real mug, insulated mug, or paper cup, you would need measured data for each condition.
%%
%[text] ## Helper Functions
%[text] These functions support the scenario colors and animation.
function rgb = coffeeTemperatureColor(temperatureF,roomTempF,initialCoffeeTempF)
normalizedTemperature = (temperatureF - roomTempF)/(initialCoffeeTempF - roomTempF);
normalizedTemperature = min(max(normalizedTemperature,0),1);
coolColor = [0.34 0.58 0.82];
warmColor = [0.90 0.58 0.20];
hotColor = [0.74 0.18 0.10];
if normalizedTemperature >= 0.5
    blendAmount = (normalizedTemperature - 0.5)/0.5;
    rgb = (1 - blendAmount)*warmColor + blendAmount*hotColor;
else
    blendAmount = normalizedTemperature/0.5;
    rgb = (1 - blendAmount)*coolColor + blendAmount*warmColor;
end
end
function palette = scenarioPalette(numberOfScenarios)
basePalette = [0.70 0.26 0.12;0.04 0.35 0.70;0.43 0.43 0.43;0.10 0.52 0.30;0.58 0.30 0.70;0.84 0.55 0.10];
if numberOfScenarios <= size(basePalette,1)
    palette = basePalette(1:numberOfScenarios,:);
else
    palette = lines(numberOfScenarios);
    palette(1:size(basePalette,1),:) = basePalette;
end
end

